// =====================================================================
// RULE:      Cognitive Services / Azure OpenAI account key listed
// SEVERITY:  Medium
// ATT&CK:    Credential Access — T1552.005 (credentials from cloud services)
// CONTROLS:  NIST 800-53 AU-6, SI-4 · NERC CIP-007-6 R4 · CIP-004-7 R4
// SOURCE:    AzureActivity  (Azure Activity connector — the free one)
// SCHEDULE:  run every 15m, look back 1h
// ENTITIES:  Account = Caller · IP = CallerIpAddress · AzureResource = _ResourceId
//
// WHY THIS RULE EXISTS
// The custom RBAC role in Lab C deliberately omits listKeys. The key is a
// bearer credential to the model endpoint: anyone holding it can call the model
// as the application, from anywhere, with no further authentication and no
// user attribution. So key retrieval is the single highest-signal action
// against this resource, and it is rare enough to alert on.
//
// This is the detection that corresponds to the control in Lab C. If the role
// boundary is ever widened, or an Owner simply bypasses it, this fires.
// =====================================================================

AzureActivity
| where TimeGenerated > ago(1h)
| where OperationNameValue has "MICROSOFT.COGNITIVESERVICES/ACCOUNTS/LISTKEYS/ACTION"
| where ActivityStatusValue in ("Success", "Succeeded", "Accepted")
| extend ResourceName = tostring(split(_ResourceId, "/")[-1])
| project
    TimeGenerated,
    Caller,
    CallerIpAddress,
    ResourceName,
    ResourceGroup,
    OperationNameValue,
    ActivityStatusValue,
    _ResourceId
| order by TimeGenerated desc

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// EXPECTED FALSE POSITIVES
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. You, retrieving the key to run the Lab F suite. This is a BENIGN TRUE
//    POSITIVE, not a false positive — the activity happened and the rule is
//    correct. Use the term precisely; it signals real SOC exposure.
// 2. A CI/CD pipeline or deployment script fetching the key at build time.
//    That is an architecture finding, not alert noise: the pipeline should use
//    a managed identity and Entra token, not a static key.
//
// TUNING — pick one, and write down why
//   (a) Narrow: exclude your own principal only when the call is from a known
//       IP and inside working hours. Keeps coverage of the stolen-session case.
//          | where not(Caller == "<your-upn>" and CallerIpAddress == "<your-ip>")
//   (b) Demote: keep the rule at Low and use it as corroboration alongside
//       other signals. Defensible when the action is genuinely routine.
//   (c) Leave it. Defensible here, because key listing should be rare.
//
// DO NOT simply exclude the account that generates the noise. That account is
// exactly the one an attacker would use.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// TRIAGE PIVOT — everything that principal did around the alert
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// AzureActivity
// | where TimeGenerated between (datetime(<alert-time>) - 2h .. datetime(<alert-time>) + 2h)
// | where Caller == "<caller from the alert>"
// | project TimeGenerated, OperationNameValue, ActivityStatusValue, _ResourceId, CallerIpAddress
// | order by TimeGenerated asc
//
// IF THIS WERE MALICIOUS — response order
//   1. Regenerate BOTH keys on the account (rotating one leaves the other live).
//   2. Revoke the principal's sessions and review its role assignments.
//   3. Check model usage and cost for the period after the listing.
//   4. Preserve the activity log for the window before anything is torn down.
